.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt FORK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fork ,
.Nm vfork ,
.Nm clone ,
.Nm clone3
.Nd create a child process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn fork "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn vfork "void"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_clone" "unsigned long flags" "void *stack" "pid_t *parent_tid" "void *tls" "pid_t *child_tid"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "SYS_clone3" "struct clone_args *args" "size_t size"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn fork
creates a child process with its own process record, address space, and
file descriptor table.
User pages are copied on write: parent and child initially share their
contents, but a write by either process does not change the other's memory.
.Pp
The child inherits the parent's program name, credentials, supplementary
groups, working directory, root directory
.Pq Xr chroot 2 ,
file creation mask, resource limits, session,
process group, nice value, dumpable flag and no_new_privs bit, and not its
parent-death signal; see
.Xr prctl 2 .
Its CPU times start at zero.
It inherits signal dispositions and the blocked signal mask;
its pending signal set is empty.
The inherited program name remains visible in
.Pa /proc/<pid>/stat
until
.Xr execve 2
replaces it.
.Pp
Inherited descriptors refer to the same open-file descriptions as the
parent's descriptors.
They share file offsets and status flags.
Parent and child can close their descriptors independently.
.Pp
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit bounds the number of threads with the parent's real user ID, the
parent's included: each thread of a process counts, as on Linux, and one
that has ended counts until it is reaped.
It bounds a new thread as it bounds a new process.
A parent with real user ID 0 is exempt.
.Pp
.Fn vfork
creates a child that shares the parent's address space instead of a copy
of it, and the parent does not return from the call until the child calls
.Xr execve 2
or ends.
Every store the child makes, on the stack included, is the parent's, so
the child should do nothing else before one of the two.
An
.Xr execve 2
that fails returns to the child, and the parent still waits.
A signal to the parent does not end its wait, except
.Dv SIGKILL ,
after which the parent dies, as Linux's killable wait lets it.
musl's and GNU libc's
.Fn vfork
make this call.
.Pp
.Fn clone ,
number 120 on i386 and 56 on x86-64, creates a child the way
.Fn fork
does, in the forms GNU libc's
.Fn fork
and both C libraries'
.Fn posix_spawn
use, or a new thread of the caller's process.
Its i386 arguments are, in order, the flags, a stack pointer for the
child, the address of the parent's copy of the child's ID, a TLS
descriptor, and the address of the child's copy.
The low byte of the flags, the signal the parent receives when the child
exits, must be
.Dv SIGCHLD
for a process; a thread sends none.
The flags may also contain
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
which gives the child the parent's address space itself, so a store, a
mapping or a
.Xr brk 2
by either is seen by both, and gives the child no alternate signal stack
unless
.Dv CLONE_VFORK
is set too;
.Dv CLONE_VFORK ,
which makes the parent wait as the parent of
.Fn vfork
waits;
.Dv CLONE_FILES ,
which gives the child the parent's descriptor table itself, so a
descriptor either opens, closes or duplicates is the other's too, and so
are the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks the table owns, which a close by either drops, until an
.Xr execve 2
succeeds, which gives the caller a copy of its own and leaves the locks
with the table; each of the two is held to its own
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
in the table they share;
.Dv CLONE_FS ,
which gives the child the parent's working directory, root directory and
file-creation mask themselves, so a
.Xr chdir 2 ,
.Xr chroot 2
or
.Xr umask 2
by either changes both, and which fails while a thread that shares them is
inside an
.Xr execve 2
that gives a caller without effective user ID 0 the IDs of a set-ID
program, since that call has chosen them on the ground that no other
process shares its directories;
.Dv CLONE_THREAD
with
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND ,
which need each other and
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
and which make the child a thread of the caller's process: it has an ID of
its own, which
.Xr gettid 2
returns, and shares the process ID, the parent, the signal dispositions,
the signals sent to the process, and the process's end, as
.Xr exit 2
and
.Xr sigaction 2
describe;
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS ,
which gives the child the TLS base of the descriptor at
.Fa tls ,
as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
takes one;
.Dv CLONE_PARENT ,
which makes the child a child of the caller's parent, as a thread is;
.Dv CLONE_PARENT_SETTID ,
which stores the child's ID at
.Fa parent_tid
in the parent's memory before the child runs;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_SETTID ,
which stores it at
.Fa child_tid
in the child's memory before the child runs;
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID ,
which records
.Fa child_tid
as the address the child's thread end clears, as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes for
.Nm set_tid_address ;
and
.Dv CLONE_DETACHED
and
.Dv CLONE_SYSVSEM ,
which are ignored, the second as a process's semaphore adjustments are its
own, which its threads share.
A failed store is ignored, as on Linux.
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWPID ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWNS ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWNET ,
.Dv CLONE_NEWUTS
and
.Dv CLONE_NEWIPC
give the child new namespaces of those kinds, as
.Xr unshare 2
describes; a child made in a new PID namespace is its init, pid 1 there,
and the parent receives its number in the parent's namespace.
A nonzero
.Fa stack
is the stack pointer the child returns to user mode with.
Every other flag is refused.
.Pp
.Fn clone3
takes the same request in Linux's
.Vt struct clone_args ,
which glibc tries before
.Fn clone
for threads and
.Xr posix_spawn 3 :
the flags, with the exit signal in a field of its own, the child and
parent thread ID addresses, the lowest address of the new stack and its
size, from whose sum the child starts, and the TLS descriptor.
.Fa size
is at least 64, the size of the structure's first version; the fields of
later versions,
.Va set_tid ,
.Va set_tid_size
and
.Va cgroup ,
must be zero.
The caller's registers are unchanged.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 2 for
.Fn fork ,
190 for
.Fn vfork ,
120 for
.Fn clone
and 435 for
.Fn clone3 .
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm fork
is call 57,
.Nm vfork
58,
.Nm clone
56 and
.Nm clone3
435;
.Nm clone
takes the child's tid address before the TLS argument, the order Linux
x86-64 has, where i386 has the TLS argument first, and the TLS argument is
the new thread's FS base.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The parent receives the child's process ID; the child receives 0.
On failure, the function returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
creates a child that exits with status 42, waits for it, and checks for a
normal exit with that status.
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
checks that a child advances the file offset seen by its parent.
.Pa tests/musl/vforkmusl.c
checks that a child made with
.Dv CLONE_VM
and
.Dv CLONE_VFORK
stores into the parent's memory before the parent runs again, that its
.Xr execve 2
leaves the parent's memory whole, that a failed one returns to the child,
and that
.Fn vfork ,
.Fn system
and
.Fn popen
run their children.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clone
was given a flag other than those above, a process an exit signal other
than
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
.Dv CLONE_THREAD
and
.Dv CLONE_SIGHAND
one without the other or without
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
or
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
a descriptor
.Xr set_thread_area 2
refuses.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clone3
was given a
.Fa size
below 64, a field of a later version that is not zero, a flag with bits
past 32, a field wider than an address, which is 32 bits on i686, an exit
signal in the flags or above 64, a stack
without a size or a size without a stack, or
.Dv CLONE_PIDFD ,
since no descriptor names a process here.
.It Bq Er E2BIG
.Fn clone3
was given a
.Fa size
above 4096, or bytes that are not zero past the structure Linux knows.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
descriptor or the
.Fn clone3
structure cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The number of threads with the parent's real user ID has reached the
parent's soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
limit, or
.Fn clone
was given
.Dv CLONE_FS
while a thread that shares the caller's directories is inside an
.Xr execve 2
that gives a caller without effective user ID 0 the IDs of a set-ID
program.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the child's process record, page tables,
user page state, kernel stack, memory region records, or descriptor table.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
.Dv CLONE_NEWNS
was given while 32 mount namespaces exist.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A
.Dv CLONE_NEW
flag other than
.Dv CLONE_NEWUSER
was given by a caller not privileged in its user namespace, or in the new
one the same call makes.
.It Bq Er EPERM
On x86-64,
.Dv CLONE_SETTLS
was given a base that is not below 0x00007ffffffff000, as
.Xr arch_prctl 2
refuses one.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.It Bq Er EUSERS
A user or PID namespace would be nested more than 7 deep.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arch_prctl 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr exit 2 ,
.Xr getpid 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr unshare 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2
